{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"d6583ee273fd8a6d","type":"group","x":620,"y":1720,"width":1380,"height":1060,"color":"3","label":"Leakage Removal"},
		{"id":"f48de0b17fb680bf","type":"group","x":440,"y":3560,"width":1636,"height":607,"color":"3","label":"Second Transmon on Resonance"},
		{"id":"802981e94346dd4c","type":"group","x":740,"y":4700,"width":1045,"height":558,"color":"3","label":"Leakage Mobility with off-resonance"},
		{"id":"8f2523594bc16b5b","type":"group","x":2260,"y":4700,"width":870,"height":611,"color":"3","label":"21 Transition With Dissipation"},
		{"id":"ef33307453b8194f","type":"group","x":1040,"y":-478,"width":440,"height":1003,"color":"3","label":"Population plots"},
		{"id":"0505476ccdd3def3","type":"group","x":2520,"y":-1080,"width":440,"height":668,"color":"3","label":"Coupler in resonance"},
		{"id":"c94ce0f8edbc7864","type":"group","x":5000,"y":-360,"width":440,"height":520,"color":"3","label":"Untitled group"},
		{"id":"ba4a9f0548c63d7f","type":"file","file":"Data/Coupler/InitialTest/dis20SlightTuning.png","x":2525,"y":940,"width":400,"height":267,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"90f1e8b004ac7d47","type":"text","text":"Qubit interaction is little large.","x":1949,"y":832,"width":247,"height":88,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"98c1ecb102ddc719","type":"file","file":"Coupler LRU/LRU With Tunable Coupler.md","x":-80,"y":-59,"width":375,"height":180,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"00ec45de1e888ae3","type":"text","text":"Is the coupler a problem to the LRU?","x":537,"y":1,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"4a45747860d85749","type":"file","file":"Data/Coupler/InitialTest/CouplerInTune.png","x":1060,"y":-458,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"e8fc3884aeb1eba2","type":"text","text":"Is the qubit too disturbed in this case? ","x":2080,"y":580,"width":231,"height":88,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"21d6f9d50a7b09d0","type":"file","file":"Data/Coupler/InitialTest/dis20NoCoupler.png","x":2525,"y":624,"width":400,"height":267,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"d990f37b9099a9e2","type":"text","text":"In the figure the coupling seems to very small, which would cause only minor decoherence with the LRU. ","x":2520,"y":463,"width":260,"height":137,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"e84bba9370380e4f","type":"file","file":"Data/Coupler/InitialTest/NoCoupler.png","x":1060,"y":-102,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"0bafe35d58b58d7c","type":"text","text":"We can slightly tune the coupler to boost leakage mobility","x":1647,"y":324,"width":250,"height":96,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"18dd5fbf1a9f44b9","type":"file","file":"Data/Coupler/InitialTest/SlightTuning.png","x":1060,"y":238,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"5f4d4ebe913ad297","type":"text","text":"Coupling turned off also stops leakage mobility.","x":1647,"y":-354,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"c098804d72dfd69f","type":"text","text":"Removing the coupler we get normal leakage mobility\n","x":1647,"y":-18,"width":250,"height":99,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"334afe91655a9485","type":"text","text":"### Fluxonium\n","x":5020,"y":-791,"width":250,"height":121},
		{"id":"ec861f30dcbb4c01","type":"file","file":"Data/Coupler/QubitMeanVsAnh/mean_population_vs_delta_U.png","x":5020,"y":-340,"width":400,"height":267,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"70bd38efaedd5f96","type":"text","text":"What is tolerable qubit population?","x":5095,"y":0,"width":250,"height":69,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"70d837bc7f596e99","type":"text","text":"We see logarithmic growth as we increase coupling, and thus the anharmonicity.","x":5583,"y":-275,"width":250,"height":137,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"8866b3c03a308b7f","type":"text","text":"In these plots the couplers leakage states are **not in resonance** with the rest of the system. How does this change the figures?","x":2080,"y":-840,"width":250,"height":169,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"84ceefe7c82fcf2c","type":"text","text":"We can detune the coupler to recover normal leakage mobility.","x":2080,"y":-18,"width":263,"height":99,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"f904bff2f1f406f5","type":"text","text":"We see leakage mobility, and the qubit coupling is turned off.","x":3160,"y":-1060,"width":250,"height":94,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"5d6c542de61fc45c","type":"text","text":"For large coupling the anharmonicity required to have the levels in resonance is very large.","x":3160,"y":-812,"width":250,"height":113,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"0cdf3a7805f8fa97","type":"text","text":"For small coupling we lose a lot of the qubit population to the coupler.","x":3160,"y":-565,"width":250,"height":111,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"8d884ff07895dae5","type":"text","text":"Can the leakage level being in tune cause problems? If they use gates which require these levels to be in resonance, this might cause unwanted population of the coupler and LRU.","x":4113,"y":35,"width":265,"height":230,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"0909e6695d3bf4ff","type":"text","text":"We should have slight detuning in the anharmonicity to have more coherent oscillation.","x":3160,"y":-320,"width":250,"height":115,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"3743205ad54d4174","type":"text","text":"We can have leakage mobility while turning of the qubit interaction, but there are several problems.","x":3680,"y":-743,"width":250,"height":143,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"cb6a82d5e0d2acf6","type":"file","file":"Data/Coupler/FLevelResonance/low_coupling/combined.png","x":2540,"y":-699,"width":400,"height":267,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"1c73967d5923e5ef","type":"file","file":"Data/Coupler/FLevelResonance/high_coupling/combined.png","x":2540,"y":-1060,"width":400,"height":267,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"5e11ba96bd4a4616","type":"text","text":"The high anharmonicity is difficult to obtain, can we have smaller anharmonicity but still good qubit population?","x":4567,"y":-350,"width":250,"height":171,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"96391dc75ca54bb2","type":"text","text":"Can we replace the coupler with something that has tunable anharmonicity?","x":4564,"y":-738,"width":250,"height":141,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"592e5a8133b0ed51","type":"text","text":"Could transmons like these be manufactured?","x":4567,"y":-1040,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"fe54fa0414e364d6","type":"text","text":"The coupling strength of the coupler determines the detuning, and the anharmonicity of the coupler.","x":4120,"y":-743,"width":250,"height":146,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"ef29e855e3ebb34b","type":"text","text":"These are not done in the computational basis (eigenbasis). Do the results still hold there?","x":4123,"y":-1022,"width":247,"height":122,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"8bd83fa144578f68","type":"text","text":"The sign of the interaction is negative, it should change whether we have the off point below or above the edge transmons.\n","x":4120,"y":-1320,"width":250,"height":180,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"c546453e72740faa","type":"file","file":"Data/Driving/DriveCouplerLRU/PositiveU/QuantumToolboxTesting/array_anh.png","x":1066,"y":1740,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"1b49ef185cd65df2","type":"file","file":"Data/Driving/DriveCouplerLRU/PositiveU/QuantumToolboxTesting/single_excitation.png","x":640,"y":1740,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"ee19790a633de1b2","type":"file","file":"Coupler LRU/ArrayLRU Anh.png","x":1480,"y":1740,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"18e2411b85a1501f","type":"text","text":"LRU does not cause dissipation in the qubit subspace.","x":640,"y":2040,"width":280,"height":93},
		{"id":"b249551709d6e8fb","type":"text","text":"Leakage removal is two orders of magnitude faster than with the array based LRU. Leakage removal is comparable to the \"filter LRU\" in *High-fidelity gates in a transmon using bath engineering for passive leakage reset*.","x":1178,"y":2040,"width":577,"height":116},
		{"id":"d0649f743f46377d","type":"text","text":"We can remove leakage by repeatedly populating the second transmon, while it has dissipation. The removal is relatively fast, rougly 300ns, where for the |20> state it is 2mus. For the coupler also in resonance, we would get 66ns removal, and for the original 3 transmon array, it would be 12mus. Here the pulses have roughly 60ns in between.","x":2280,"y":4720,"width":352,"height":258},
		{"id":"fd6ab8694ed1a260","type":"file","file":"Data/LRU/CLRU/NoCouplerTuning/res_diss_pulse_10.png","x":2280,"y":5022,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"38a36115932d55d2","type":"file","file":"Data/LRU/CLRU/NoCouplerTuning/res_diss.png","x":2710,"y":4720,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"ef6c6522c39289e9","type":"text","text":"The leakage mobility is very fast when there is one excitation in the second transmon. We could take advantage of this and populate the second transmon on purpose.","x":1273,"y":3720,"width":369,"height":140},
		{"id":"e44ed3d6bb2572fb","type":"text","text":"Setting the second transmon on resonance returns leakage mobility. It is more than order of magnitude slower than having the coupler in resonance.","x":1284,"y":3588,"width":346,"height":126},
		{"id":"d49392911143c741","type":"file","file":"Data/LRU/CLRU/NoCouplerTuning/res_21_pulse.png","x":1273,"y":3956,"width":287,"height":191},
		{"id":"5676cd254a006127","type":"file","file":"Data/LRU/CLRU/NoCouplerTuning/res_diss_pulse_20.png","x":2710,"y":5024,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"1ce89619c313c232","type":"file","file":"Data/Driving/DriveCouplerLRU/PositiveU/QuantumToolboxTesting/array_anh.png","x":1656,"y":3580,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"e1c506ca76b5649d","type":"file","file":"Data/LRU/CLRU/NoCouplerTuning/res_diss.png","x":1656,"y":3880,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"effb5c03da074d52","type":"file","file":"Data/LRU/CLRU/NoCouplerTuning/off_res_state10.png","x":758,"y":4718,"width":368,"height":245,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"2b05d86184ec9e98","type":"text","text":"The off-resonance halts leakage mobility, and we cannot use off-resonant transmons for leakage removal. Additionally, in an off-resonant grid, leakage will be localized, only moving due to two qubit gates.","x":1110,"y":5420,"width":309,"height":221,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"77c19e019bbe8f4b","type":"text","text":"Due to the off-resonance used, there is no practical leakage mobility.","x":1537,"y":4718,"width":230,"height":88,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"cd6e93f3362d3046","type":"file","file":"Data/LRU/CLRU/NoCouplerTuning/off_res_state11.png","x":1126,"y":4718,"width":368,"height":245,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"1430975628f60237","type":"file","file":"Data/LRU/CLRU/NoCouplerTuning/off_res_state21.png","x":1126,"y":4994,"width":368,"height":245,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"df8b9812e75b703a","type":"file","file":"Data/LRU/CLRU/NoCouplerTuning/off_res_state20.png","x":758,"y":4994,"width":368,"height":245,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"126406d2a32fa9ff","type":"text","text":"To remove leakage we could populate the second transmons to the |1> state. If we use just dissipation, can we remove leakage?","x":1756,"y":4340,"width":300,"height":149,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"58dc539a57793b14","type":"file","file":"Data/LRU/CLRU/NoCouplerTuning/res_state10.png","x":460,"y":3580,"width":400,"height":267,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"7ef8291a1f935e72","type":"file","file":"Data/LRU/CLRU/NoCouplerTuning/res_state11.png","x":860,"y":3580,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"ab82ac1d91eb5a5d","type":"file","file":"Data/Driving/DriveCouplerLRU/PositiveU/PauliString/lru_vs_no_lru/gaussian/anh_comparison.png","x":1467,"y":2260,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"bedd8d69696da2ec","type":"text","text":"Coupler worsens gate fidelity. But the dissipation of the LRU does not cause this, the worse dissipation comes from the tunable coupler. Since tunable couplers are used, the gate fidelities should not be worse than in that kind of tunable coupler coupled transmon qubit setups.","x":1040,"y":2560,"width":401,"height":220},
		{"id":"6b291d26049cc54b","type":"file","file":"Data/Driving/DriveCouplerLRU/PositiveU/PauliString/lru_vs_no_lru/gaussian/proj_comparison.png","x":1041,"y":2260,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"8428e75f42a12937","type":"text","text":"[Leakage RB](https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.03081)","x":840,"y":2300,"width":149,"height":60},
		{"id":"beb8e8f38b4b5b32","type":"file","file":"Data/LRU/CLRU/NoCouplerTuning/res_state21.png","x":860,"y":3880,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"5e355df82d9eeb49","type":"file","file":"Data/LRU/CLRU/NoCouplerTuning/res_state20.png","x":460,"y":3880,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"389b5af7d4d271b0","type":"text","text":"In typical transmon setups, neighbouring transmons are off-resonce. Can we use transmons as is for leakage removal, or do we need to tune them?","x":1136,"y":4340,"width":250,"height":177,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"77c7ab2b4ea48b90","type":"text","text":"For 14ns pulses with 4ns pauses in between, the coupler LRU is able to removal all the leakage.","x":1467,"y":2560,"width":314,"height":90},
		{"id":"0fa584c831458724","type":"text","text":"We cannot achieve the required anharmonicity for the coupler with the traditional transmon tunable coupler. The transmon where we do the leakage removal is out of the computations, so we do not have any limit for what its frequency can be. What if we set it to be on resonant with the other transmon?","x":1084,"y":3080,"width":315,"height":260,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"0fa52b2208565353","type":"file","file":"Coupler LRU/Marxer et al_2023_Long-distance transmon coupler with CZ gate fidelity above $99.pdf","x":2512,"y":1500,"width":550,"height":280,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"dba08427dbc26166","type":"text","text":"In the IQM paper this is enough for them to realize gates. Also it is strong in the sense that it is worth turning off with the coupler.","x":2525,"y":1280,"width":262,"height":146,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"f8390b69af4efb3f","type":"text","text":"What is the exact problem the coupling causes?","x":3000,"y":1301,"width":244,"height":105,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"cb1f62e5c7618015","type":"text","text":"These results do not well show how good the LRU is in removing leakage. We could simulate leakage by faulty single qubit gates, and see how does our LRU keep it maintained over several gates.","x":1160,"y":1207,"width":250,"height":277,"color":"1"}
	],
